 in entry-level work and is assessed through practical scenarios and interviews.","concept_explanation":"Principle: Stakeholder analysis identifies influence, needs and decision rights; elicitation gathers evidence through suitable collaborative methods. Example: Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process. Workplace method: Map stakeholders, plan questions, conduct interviews or workshops, observe work and confirm findings. Example: Use Stakeholder map, interview guide and workshop notes and retain evidence of the result. Quality and interview check: Verify the outcome, explain trade-offs and avoid: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth. Example: Which elicitation technique would you choose for conflicting requirements and why?","key_terms":[{"term":"Stakeholder Management","definition":"A practical capability used in stakeholders and elicitation work.","example":"Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process."},{"term":"Requirements","definition":"A practical capability used in stakeholders and elicitation work.","example":"Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process."}]},"principles":{"rules":[{"rule_number":1,"title":"Principle","explanation":"Stakeholder analysis identifies influence, needs and decision rights; elicitation gathers evidence through suitable collaborative methods.","why_it_works":"Example: Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process.","exception":null},{"rule_number":2,"title":"Workplace method","explanation":"Map stakeholders, plan questions, conduct interviews or workshops, observe work and confirm findings.","why_it_works":"Example: Use Stakeholder map, interview guide and workshop notes and retain evidence of the result.","exception":null},{"rule_number":3,"title":"Quality and interview check","explanation":"Verify the outcome, explain trade-offs and avoid: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth.","why_it_works":"Example: Which elicitation technique would you choose for conflicting requirements and why?","exception":null}],"important_distinctions":[]},"application_method":{"method_title":"Learn, configure, validate","method_overview":"Connect the idea to a practical workflow, then validate the outcome.","steps":[{"step_number":1,"title":"Understand","instruction":"Identify the process goal, actors, inputs and expected outcome.","reason":"A clear process prevents incorrect work.","student_check":"Can you explain the goal in one sentence?"},{"step_number":2,"title":"Apply","instruction":"Complete this workplace lab: Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process.","reason":"Hands-on execution turns knowledge into a reusable skill.","student_check":"Did you complete every required part?"},{"step_number":3,"title":"Validate","instruction":"Test the result, review the evidence and correct any gap.","reason":"Validation makes the work reliable and interview-ready.","student_check":"Does the result satisfy the stated outcome?"}],"use_when":"Use this workflow for assess work.","do_not_use_when":"Do not expose confidential information or skip verification.","faster_method":"Avoid this common mistake: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth."},"worked_examples":[{"example_number":1,"title":"Guided practice","difficulty":"Foundation","question_or_scenario":"Complete this workplace lab: Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process.","given_information":["Stakeholder Management","Requirements"],"reasoning_steps":[{"step_number":1,"explanation":"Review the assess requirements and define the expected result."},{"step_number":2,"explanation":"Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process."},{"step_number":3,"explanation":"Validate the outcome. Avoid this common mistake: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth."}],"final_answer":"A complete submission uses Stakeholder map, interview guide and workshop notes, follows the workflow, records validation evidence and explains one limitation.","why_the_answer_is_correct":"The result follows the defined process, covers the required elements and includes a validation step.","learning_takeaway":"Avoid this common mistake: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth."}],"usage_comparisons":[],"common_mistakes":[{"mistake_number":1,"title":"Acting before understanding","incorrect_approach":"Starting immediately without confirming scope, inputs and success criteria.","why_students_make_it":"Speed can feel more important than process discipline.","why_it_is_wrong":"An incomplete process creates unreliable results and avoidable rework.","correction":"Define the outcome, complete the workflow, and validate it once.","prevention_tip":"Avoid this common mistake: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth."}],"memory_support":{"memory_aid":"Avoid this common mistake: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth.","mental_model":"Concept → Practice → Proof","quick_recall_note":"Understand → Apply → Validate"},"placement_support":{"question_identification_signals":["Assess","Stakeholders and Elicitation: Workplace Lab & Module Check","Stakeholder Management","Requirements"],"time_management_tip":"Avoid this common mistake: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth.","exam_traps":["Skipping context","Ignoring edge cases or validation"],"interview_relevance":"Use the completed activity and its validation evidence to explain your approach in interviews."},"checkpoint_questions":[{"question_number":1,"type":"application","question":"In your own words, what problem does stakeholders and elicitation solve?","answer":"Stakeholder analysis identifies influence, needs and decision rights; elicitation gathers evidence through suitable collaborative methods.","explanation":"A strong answer states the business or user problem before naming a tool.","skill_tested":"Stakeholders and Elicitation: Workplace Lab & Module Check","difficulty":"Foundation"},{"question_number":2,"type":"reasoning","question":"How would you apply this capability in the following workplace situation: Students, faculty, administrators and finance have different needs in a course-assignment process.","answer":"Map stakeholders, plan questions, conduct interviews or workshops, observe work and confirm findings.","explanation":"The response should describe a controlled, verifiable workflow rather than only a feature.","skill_tested":"Stakeholders and Elicitation: Workplace Lab & Module Check","difficulty":"Foundation"},{"question_number":3,"type":"application","question":"Which elicitation technique would you choose for conflicting requirements and why?","answer":"Use Stakeholder analysis identifies influence, needs and decision rights; elicitation gathers evidence through suitable collaborative methods. Then describe the workflow: Map stakeholders, plan questions, conduct interviews or workshops, observe work and confirm findings.","explanation":"Interview answers should connect principle, action, validation and outcome.","skill_tested":"Stakeholders and Elicitation: Workplace Lab & Module Check","difficulty":"Foundation"},{"question_number":4,"type":"application","question":"Identify the main risk in this incorrect approach: Asking leading questions or treating the loudest stakeholder as the only source of truth.","answer":"The approach is unreliable because it breaks the controls implied by Stakeholder analysis iden